  "body": "**Status: research note and scoped negative result (bit supplier). No new \u03ba, no supplier change, and no selected-record edit.**\n\n## Question\n\nPR1084 (`research/bit-orthogonal-pair-decoders`, navinor) showed that a decoder term with orthogonal punctured reader and source lines costs 6 instead of 7. It reached P102 (cst 54) over all 120 lines, but left two open points:\n- At nine stages, the inventory R = 439 exceeds the hosting wall 4v = 408.\n- The shipped PR589 puncture admits only one such pair (P110).\n\nThis PR asks a narrower question. Inside the family that actually built the selected bit word (PR555's `gen_p3` on PR543's frozen L3 plan), how many cost-6 terms can a legal reader set reach? And what are the resulting units worth at the nine-stage template and at PR555's eleven-stage template, once hosting, idle children and the generator's real helper inventory are all priced?\n\n## Result\n\n1. **P102 is unreachable in this family; the maximum is j = 5 (P106).**\n   - The frozen plan has 19 eligible pseudo-target lines. Of the C(18,9) = 48,620 subsets, 17,478 are admissible.\n   - The root-dual count j is distributed `{0:4515, 1:7975, 2:3977, 3:929, 4:77, 5:5}`.\n   - The shipped P has j = 1 (pair 66\u201387, i.e. PR1084's P110).\n   - A second implementation, written before reading the first, agrees on all 127 compared fields.\n2. **At R = 439, eleven stages would look favorable, but that premise is false.**\n   - Eleven stages host every unit whole (11R \u2264 64v).\n   - At R = 439 the j = 5 sets would gain +1.94% to +1.97% over the selected bit c.\n   - Real `gen_p3` builds of 4,989 reader sets need R(P) = 446\u2013472 helpers, against 439 for the shipped set. PR555's `choose.py` minimised exactly these extra carriers.\n   - With their own R(P), all 82 j \u2265 4 sets price between **\u22122.78% and \u22120.21%**.\n   - None of the 2,785 built j = 2/3 sets passes the nine-stage wall (min R 446 > 436 for j = 2; 448 > 432 for j = 3).\n3. **The only hostable gain is the shipped P110 at nine stages, +0.70%.** That is below PR1084's recorded P104 line (+1.3294%) and below the same P104 gain re-based to the current selected complex b (+1.9829%, a stated transfer premise). The bit side does not bind today; its headroom is 3.46%.\n\nExact parts:\n- the census;\n- template parameters and walls;\n- the certified idle optima and window max-flow;\n- every c, as a certified lower bound from the pinned selected-record engine.\n\nPositive controls reproduce, exactly:\n- PR589 at nine stages and at eleven stages;\n- PR555 at eleven stages;\n- a byte-for-byte rebuild of PR555's word.\n\n## Premises and open obligations\n\n- No P(111\u2212j) word with punctured duals is built, and `gen_p3` does not model dead non-reader lines.\n- The asymmetric rank-6 COPY (PR620) is unrealised.\n- Profiles are PR589-based proxies with an average-helper shift for R \u2260 439. The margin to the nearest threshold is at least 1.5 percentage points.\n- The receiver search is bounded, not a proof of minimal R.\n- j = 2/3 coverage is time-capped at 281/929 and 2,504/3,977 sets, with a two-receiver screen over all 4,906.\n- Eleven-stage use would need a new assembly receipt.\n- The census fixes the label-reader basis.\n\nThis is a finite result for the frozen plan, not an impossibility theorem for orthogonal-pair decoders. The README lists three open next questions: a jointly optimised plan, realising the shipped P110 word, and pricing the built-word profiles.\n\n## Reproduce\n\nFrom the repository root (Python 3.10+, standard library only, about 100 s):\n\n```sh\npython3 -I -B research/bit-orthogonal-pair-stage-screen/screen.py --check\n```\n\nExpected last line begins `PASS_BIT_ORTHOGONAL_PAIR_STAGE_SCREEN rows=44 enum11=82 ...` and ends `| own_R: L11 j>=4 82/82 hostable, >=P104 0/82, >=rebased 0/82, best -0.211302% at R=451 ... checks=57`. It runs `census.py --check`, `independent_census.py` and `price.py`, and requires each of their PASS lines.\n\nThe build receipt `CARRIERS.json.gz` is regenerated by `carriers.py`. That needs numpy (2.5.4, verified PyPI wheel) and took 1,142 s on 4 workers; the README gives the exact command.\n\nChecks run locally:\n- All four scripts pass on CPython 3.14.4.\n- `scripts/research_package.py --check-only` gives `PASS_PACKAGE_METADATA_AND_PINS`.\n- The 65 `test_research_*.py` tests pass (2 skipped).\n- The registered Python 3.12 replay was **not** run, because 3.12 is not installed here. The scripts parse under the 3.10 grammar.\n\n## Provenance and credit\n\n- **Base and refresh:** base `0fdd7d82de03e43c9e5a1000dfbcd380d1b687d2`, priority revision 4. Canonical main was unchanged at the submission checkpoint (2026-10-15 00:09 UTC).\n- **Overlap:** the refresh at 23:54 UTC covered 447 open PRs and 902 discussion records with complete pagination. It found no P102/P110 realisation or stage-count pricing.\n- **Track and claim:** bit supplier, OS seed 4. No sprint coordination issue exists, so the claim was recorded locally.\n- **Scope:** `TASK.json` declares a changed scope relative to the bit cards (register models / H2-K), with the reason.\n- **Credit:**\n  - navinor: PR1084.\n  - eumemic: PR555 generator, PR543 plan.\n  - DreamingOfClouds: PR545, PR589, PR595, PR717.\n  - PR442/PR510 hosting and the nine-stage chain; PR493 eleven-stage chain.\n  - Joel Pulikkan / GamingPuzzled: PR620.\n  - Russell Seligmann and Cody Harris: PR757, PR761.\n  - themilkman216: PR968 P104.\n\nPrepared for Sagar Thakrar with substantial AI assistance (Claude Code, Anthropic). Subagents wrote the census, pricer, screen and build driver. A separate agent re-implemented the census blind. An adversarial review found the R-sensitivity that motivated the real builds. The main session reran every command. Apache-2.0.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
